[size=5][b]revd: more engine audio slots for GTA IV: The Complete Edition[/b][/size]
[i]A research release. Source, figures and issues: [url=https://github.com/gutbash/revd]github.com/gutbash/revd[/url][/i]

[size=4][b]Abstract[/b][/size]
GTA IV allocates one looping engine sound per distinct vehicle [b]model[/b] within earshot, not per vehicle, and stores those allocations in a fixed twenty-five entry table. The limit is invisible in a clean install because stock traffic rarely presents twenty-five distinct models at once; it becomes obvious with a traffic pack, where models past the twenty-fifth are given no slot and produce no engine sound at all. The table cannot be extended in place, because unrelated statics occupy the memory immediately after it. revd relocates the table into its own image, repoints all fourteen absolute references to it, raises the probe loop's bound, and raises the physical audio heap the slot block is carved from. The heap raise is not optional: at thirty slots on the stock heap the game crashes during audio initialisation.

[size=4][b]Problem[/b][/size]
The symptom reads as a broken audio mod. Cars roll past in silence while others nearby sound normal, and which cars are silent changes as traffic churns. Nothing is broken. The game has run out of places to put the sound.

The audio init at RVA 0x58D4C3 to 0x58D512 probes STREAM_ENGINE_1 upwards by name and stores each resolved slot into a table of sixteen-byte entries at 0xE832B8, with the count at 0xE83298. The loop terminates on:
[code]0x58D50D    cmp edi, 0x19        twenty-five[/code]
Because the probe is by name, the ceiling is jointly enforced by the executable and by pc/audio/config/waveslots.xml. Raising one without the other achieves nothing.

[size=4][b]Method[/b][/size]
[b]Relocation.[/b] Every reference to the table is an absolute displacement onto entry zero's fields, at offsets 0, 4, 8 and 12. There are fourteen, spread across 0x58B979 to 0x828054. revd allocates a sixty-four entry table inside its own image, sixteen-byte aligned behind a thirty-two byte header, and rewrites all fourteen displacements. All fourteen are verified against their expected values first; if any one disagrees, nothing is written.

[b]Bound.[/b] The imm8 at 0x58D50F becomes the configured slot count.

[b]Heap.[/b] The physical audio heap is a single imm32 at 0x4C158C, stock 126 MB. revd patches it to the configured size, 192 MB by default.

All three writes must land after .text is decrypted and before the audio system initialises. revd polls that window at 50 ms, verifies each site, and if the window closes first it leaves the game stock and records that it did.

[size=4][b]Findings[/b][/size]
Three observations, one machine, 2026-09-06:
[list]
[*]25 slots on the stock 126 MB heap: runs.
[*]30 slots on the stock 126 MB heap: crashes during audio initialisation.
[*]64 slots on a 192 MB heap: runs.
[/list]
One engine slot is declared as 794,624 bytes in waveslots.xml, so sixty-four declare 48.5 MB against 19.0 MB at stock.

[b]The heap requirement is observed, not derived.[/b] The other seventy slots in waveslots.xml declare 129.2 MB between them, which already exceeds the stock 126 MB heap on its own. The heap is therefore not a straight sum of declared sizes, and what the allocator actually reserves was never measured. The crash threshold between twenty-five and thirty slots was not bisected. Treat 192 MB as a value known to work, not as a computed requirement.

[b]Sixty-four is a ceiling, not a guarantee.[/b] It means the game can voice sixty-four distinct models simultaneously. Whether it does depends on how much variety is actually present.

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Have an ASI loader present. Ultimate ASI Loader as dinput8.dll is the usual one, and if you run FusionFix you already have it.
[*]Put revd.asi and revd.ini in your GTAIV folder, next to GTAIV.exe.
[*][b]Extend waveslots.xml.[/b] See below. Skipping this makes the plugin do nothing useful.
[*]Launch. revd.log records every patch attempt and its outcome.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Step 3, the part people miss[/b][/size]
The game probes for slots [b]by name[/b], so a slot waveslots.xml does not define stays empty and raising the bound alone buys nothing at all.

[b]The easy way.[/b] Download add-engine-slots.ps1 from the GitHub repository and run it. It appends the missing entries to your existing file rather than replacing it, so another audio mod's changes survive, it backs the original up to waveslots.xml.bak, and it is safe to run twice:
[code].\add-engine-slots.ps1 -Slots 64[/code]
The script is hosted on GitHub rather than bundled in the download, because Nexus's automated checks quarantine any archive containing a PowerShell script.

[b]The manual way.[/b] Open pc\audio\config\waveslots.xml and, for every number from 26 to 64, add a block like this before the closing tag, changing only the number:
[code]  <Slot>
    <Name content="ascii">STREAM_ENGINE_26</Name>
    <MaxHeaderSize value="2048" />
    <LoadType content="ascii">BANK</LoadType>
    <Size value="794624" />
  </Slot>[/code]
Back the file up first.

[size=4][b]Verifying[/b][/size]
revd.log is the source of truth. Two lines mean it worked:
[code]engine slots: table relocated to 0F2A0120 (14 sites), probe bound 25 -> 64
audio heap: 126 MB -> 192 MB[/code]
If either is missing, the log names the check that failed.

[size=4][b]Limitations[/b][/size]
These are the boundaries of what was tested. Nothing outside them should be assumed to work.
[list]
[*][b]Complete Edition 1.2.0.59 only.[/b] Every address is a hardcoded offset for that exact build. On any other version verification fails, nothing is patched, and the log says so.
[*][b]Sixty-four is a hard ceiling[/b], set by the relocated table's capacity. Higher values are clamped.
[*][b]The heap figure is empirical.[/b] It is a value known to work, not a derived one.
[*][b]The heap raise is mandatory above roughly thirty slots.[/b] Raising the count without it crashes during audio initialisation. revd warns about that combination at load.
[*][b]The patch window is narrow.[/b] An ASI loader loads early enough. Later injection may not, and revd will detect that and leave the game alone.
[*][b]No audio quality or mixing analysis.[/b] Whether sixty-four simultaneous engine loops changes the mix balance, or costs measurable CPU, was not investigated.
[*][b]One machine, one mod stack.[/b] Developed against a single install with FusionFix loaded through an ASI loader.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Reproducibility[/b][/size]
Anyone reproducing this should report game build, slot count, heap size and the contents of revd.log. The open questions are the exact crash threshold on the stock heap, what the audio allocator actually reserves per slot at runtime as opposed to what the config declares, and whether the relocated table has any effect on audio CPU cost.

[size=4][b]Verifying what you downloaded[/b][/size]
The full source is on GitHub under MIT, and every binary is built from that source by public GitHub Actions, so you can read the build log or build it yourself rather than taking the upload on trust. It is a single translation unit with no dependencies beyond the Win32 SDK.

revd.asi SHA-256 0e76e726e294eaf861d497bb77d0c12b87137a219124a297065e53c597d3d50d

[size=4][b]Companion release[/b][/size]
[url=https://github.com/gutbash/popctl]popctl[/url] moves the pedestrian and traffic removal radius, which is a separate limit with a separate fix. Raising traffic variety with popctl is exactly what drives you into the ceiling this release lifts, so the two are worth reading together.

[size=4][b]License[/b][/size]
MIT. Do what you like with it, including shipping it inside your own mod pack.
